\title{The Franke Decomposition for Level-One Spherical Automorphic Forms and the Pole Geometry of the Standard Eisenstein Series}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-01}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the spectral decomposition of spherical automorphic forms on the modular
surface $X = \SL(2,\ZZ)\backslash\HH$ and separate its two structural
ingredients: an algebraic skeleton and an analytic engine. On the algebraic side
we isolate the linear-algebraic core of the Franke Decomposition Theorem in the
level-one spherical case, namely that the space of automorphic forms is the
internal direct sum of the cusp forms and the finite-dimensional span of the
Laurent coefficients of the standard Eisenstein series $E(s;z)$ at its poles. This
yields existence, uniqueness, and finiteness of the decomposition with no
analytic hypotheses beyond complementarity. On the analytic side we pin down the
unique pole of $E(s;z)$ in $\Re(s)\ge\tfrac12$: a simple pole at $s=1$, produced
entirely by the arithmetic factor $\zeta(2s-1)$ of the scattering matrix, with
residue exactly $\tfrac12$. We prove the pole is genuine, so the Eisenstein
contribution is nonzero. Together these results certify both the finiteness and
the nontriviality of the residual term and trace them to the single simple pole
of the Riemann zeta function.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The spectral theory of automorphic forms decomposes the space of functions on an
arithmetic quotient into structurally distinguished pieces. In its general form,
Franke's theorem describes the space of automorphic forms on an arithmetic
quotient of a reductive group as a direct sum indexed by cuspidal support. The
theorem is famously analytic, resting on the meromorphic continuation and
functional equations of Eisenstein series.

We treat the smallest genuinely interesting case --- spherical automorphic forms
of level one on the modular surface --- and make two points. First, the
combinatorial shape of the decomposition (existence, uniqueness, finiteness) is
pure linear algebra: it is the assertion that the cuspidal subspace and the span
of finitely many Laurent coefficients of the standard Eisenstein series are
complementary. Second, the reason the Laurent span is finite and nontrivial is
arithmetic and localizes to a single point: $E(s;z)$ has exactly one pole in
$\Re(s)\ge\tfrac12$, at $s=1$, produced by $\zeta(2s-1)$, with residue $\tfrac12$.

\section{The setting}

\subsection{The modular surface}
Let $\HH = \{z = x+iy : y>0\}$ carry the hyperbolic metric
$ds^2 = (dx^2+dy^2)/y^2$. The group
\[
\SL(2,\ZZ) = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d\end{pmatrix} : a,b,c,d\in\ZZ,\ ad-bc=1 \right\}
\]
acts by $\gamma z = (az+b)/(cz+d)$, and the quotient
$X = \SL(2,\ZZ)\backslash\HH$ is a finite-area hyperbolic orbifold with a single
cusp at $y\to\infty$.

\subsection{Spherical automorphic forms and cusp forms}

\begin{definition}[Spherical automorphic form]
A \emph{spherical automorphic form} is a smooth $f:\HH\to\CC$ invariant under
$\SL(2,\ZZ)$, an eigenfunction of the hyperbolic Laplacian
$\Delta = -y^2(\partial_x^2+\partial_y^2)$, with at most polynomial growth in the
cusp. Let $V$ be the space of such forms in a fixed spectral window.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cusp form]
A form $f$ is a \emph{cusp form} if $\int_0^1 f(x+iy)\,dx = 0$ for all $y>0$;
equivalently, $f$ decays rapidly as $y\to\infty$. The cusp forms form a subspace
$\cusp\subseteq V$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The standard Eisenstein series}

\begin{definition}[Standard Eisenstein series]
For $\Re(s)>1$,
\[
E(s;z) = \sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_\infty\backslash\SL(2,\ZZ)} \big(\Imag\,\gamma z\big)^s,
\]
where $\Gamma_\infty$ stabilizes the cusp. It continues meromorphically to
$s\in\CC$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Constant term]\label{prop:const}
The constant Fourier coefficient of $E(s;z)$ along the cusp is
\[
\int_0^1 E(s;x+iy)\,dx = y^s + \varphi(s)\,y^{1-s},
\qquad
\varphi(s) = \sqrt{\pi}\,\frac{\Gamma\!\left(s-\tfrac12\right)\zeta(2s-1)}{\Gamma(s)\,\zeta(2s)}.
\]
\end{proposition}

In $\Re(s)\ge\tfrac12$ the ratio $\Gamma(s-\tfrac12)/\Gamma(s)$ is holomorphic and
nonzero and $\zeta(2s)$ is holomorphic and nonvanishing, so the only possible pole
of $\varphi$ comes from $\zeta(2s-1)$, whose argument equals $1$ exactly at $s=1$.

\section{The analytic core: the pole at \texorpdfstring{$s=1$}{s=1}}

Our analytic input is the residue of the Riemann zeta function,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zeta}
\lim_{u\to1}(u-1)\,\zeta(u) = 1,
\end{equation}
a limit over the punctured neighborhood of $1$ in $\CC$.

\begin{lemma}[Punctured-neighborhood transport]\label{lem:transport}
The affine map $\sigma(s)=2s-1$ tends to $1$ along the punctured neighborhood
filter at $1$: it maps points near but distinct from $1$ to points near but
distinct from $1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Continuity gives $\sigma(s)\to\sigma(1)=1$ for the unpunctured filter.
Injectivity of $\sigma$ (if $2s-1=1$ then $s=1$) shows $s\ne1\Rightarrow\sigma(s)\ne1$,
preserving the punctured restriction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Residue of the arithmetic scattering factor]\label{thm:res}
As $s\to1$,
\[
(s-1)\,\zeta(2s-1) \longrightarrow \tfrac12.
\]
Equivalently, $\zeta(2s-1)$ has a simple pole at $s=1$ with residue $\tfrac12$,
the sole source of the pole of $E(s;z)$ in $\Re(s)\ge\tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Composing \eqref{eq:zeta} with the substitution $u=2s-1$ of
Lemma~\ref{lem:transport} gives $\big((2s-1)-1\big)\zeta(2s-1)\to1$, i.e.
$2(s-1)\zeta(2s-1)\to1$. Multiplying by $\tfrac12$ yields
$(s-1)\zeta(2s-1)\to\tfrac12$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Genuine blow-up]\label{thm:blowup}
The function $s\mapsto\zeta(2s-1)$ has no finite limit as $s\to1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\zeta(2s-1)\to L\in\CC$, then since $s-1\to0$ we would have
$(s-1)\zeta(2s-1)\to0\cdot L=0$; but Theorem~\ref{thm:res} gives $\tfrac12$. By
uniqueness of limits along the (nontrivial) punctured filter, $0=\tfrac12$, a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$E(s;z)$ has a genuine pole at $s=1$; the residual Eisenstein contribution to the
decomposition of $V$ has nonzero residue and is present.
\end{corollary}

\section{The algebraic skeleton: the decomposition}

Let $V$ be a $\CC$-vector space (automorphic forms), $\cusp\subseteq V$ a subspace
(cusp forms), and $\ell_1,\dots,\ell_n\in V$ a finite family (Laurent
coefficients of $E(s;z)$ at its poles). Put $\Eis=\spann_\CC\{\ell_1,\dots,\ell_n\}$.

\begin{definition}[Complementarity]
$\cusp$ and $\Eis$ are \emph{complementary} if
$\cusp\cap\Eis=\{0\}$ and $\cusp+\Eis=V$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]\label{thm:exist}
If $\cusp$ and $\Eis$ are complementary, every $f\in V$ can be written
$f = c + \sum_{i=1}^n a_i\ell_i$ with $c\in\cusp$ and $a_i\in\CC$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$V=\cusp+\Eis$ gives $f=c+e$ with $e\in\Eis$, and $e=\sum_i a_i\ell_i$ by
definition of the span.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]\label{thm:unique}
Under the same hypothesis, the parts $c$ and $\sum_i a_i\ell_i$ are uniquely
determined by $f$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $c+e=c'+e'$ then $c-c'=e'-e\in\cusp\cap\Eis=\{0\}$, so $c=c'$ and $e=e'$. This
is uniqueness of coordinates in an internal direct sum; no analytic normalization
is used.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finiteness]\label{thm:finite}
$\Eis$ is finite-dimensional with $\dim\Eis\le n$; the Eisenstein part is a finite
linear combination.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Eis$ is spanned by $n$ vectors. Finiteness of the generating family is the
analytic fact that $E(s;z)$ has finitely many poles in the region --- one, at
$s=1$, by Theorems~\ref{thm:res}--\ref{thm:blowup}.
\end{proof}

Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:exist}--\ref{thm:finite}, $V=\cusp\oplus\Eis$ is an
internal direct sum with $\Eis$ finite-dimensional: the level-one spherical Franke
decomposition.

\section{Why level one gives a single standard Eisenstein series}

\begin{proposition}[Uniqueness of the trivial character]
The number of Dirichlet characters of conductor $1$ is exactly $1$: only the
trivial character.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Characters modulo $1$ are characters of the trivial group $(\ZZ/1\ZZ)^\times$,
which has one element.
\end{proof}

Hence no nontrivial twist is available and the single untwisted $E(s;z)$ governs
the whole Eisenstein spectrum at level one.

\section{Synthesis}

Finiteness of $\Eis$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:finite}) needs finitely many poles of
$E(s;z)$; Proposition~\ref{prop:const} and Theorem~\ref{thm:res} give exactly one,
at $s=1$, from the pole of $\zeta$. Nontriviality follows from the nonzero residue
$\tfrac12$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:blowup}). Existence and uniqueness
(Theorems~\ref{thm:exist}--\ref{thm:unique}) are pure complementarity. Thus the
simple pole of $\zeta$ at $1$ controls both the size and the presence of the
residual automorphic spectrum on the modular surface.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Residue rationality across the modular tower.} For every principal
congruence subgroup, each pole of the standard Eisenstein series in the closed
right half-plane should carry a residue that is a rational multiple of the
reciprocal of the covolume of the quotient surface: the pole comes solely from
the $\zeta$-driven arithmetic factor of residue $\tfrac12$, and geometric
constants enter only as rational scalings.

\textbf{Uniform bound on the residual dimension.} The dimension of the residual
part of the spherical spectrum for a congruence quotient should be bounded by the
number of cusps, independently of level: each cusp contributes one Eisenstein
family with one boundary pole.

\textbf{Rigidity of the splitting.} The cusp/Eisenstein decomposition should be
unique even before any growth or integrability condition, since complementarity
alone forces it; the analytic hypotheses classically invoked are sufficient but
not necessary.

\section{Conclusion}

The level-one spherical Franke decomposition separates into an unconditional
algebraic skeleton and an analytic engine. The engine reduces to a single fact:
the standard Eisenstein series on the modular surface has exactly one pole in
$\Re(s)\ge\tfrac12$, a simple pole at $s=1$ of residue $\tfrac12$, inherited from
the Riemann zeta function.

